\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Left:} the mining mechanism as currently witnessed. Solid edges carry certificates (labels name the gate, the count, or the hazard whose repair the edge depends on). \emph  {Dashed nodes and edges are the open gaps.} S5 comprehension is drawn dashed into the field-scale account because its strategy half has no producer in the pipeline (\textsf  {H13}), so its verdict cannot discriminate; S11's canon is dashed because it computes and then discards. Note that the \emph  {learning} path (S3 $\to $ S10 $\to $ S12) became solid only after \textsf  {H14}, \textsf  {H15}, \textsf  {H18} and \textsf  {H19} were closed --- before that it either aborted on an id, printed into a void, or measured a different corpus. \textbf  {Right:} A13 as a selection diagram. $S$ switches corpus scale (12 papers $\to $ the full field); ``the pipeline completes at field scale'' is identified only if the $S$-dependence can be separated from the pipeline edge. It cannot yet: \textsf  {H20} was a defect whose \emph  {severity} is scale-dependent (a $23\times $ waste here, ${\sim }138$k redundant rebuilds and non-termination at field scale), which is precisely an $S$-to-outcome path that does not pass through the pipeline edge. Scale transport is therefore a derivation obligation, not an extrapolation.}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dag}{{1}{5}{\textbf {Left:} the mining mechanism as currently witnessed. Solid edges carry certificates (labels name the gate, the count, or the hazard whose repair the edge depends on). \emph {Dashed nodes and edges are the open gaps.} S5 comprehension is drawn dashed into the field-scale account because its strategy half has no producer in the pipeline (\hz {H13}), so its verdict cannot discriminate; S11's canon is dashed because it computes and then discards. Note that the \emph {learning} path (S3 $\to $ S10 $\to $ S12) became solid only after \hz {H14}, \hz {H15}, \hz {H18} and \hz {H19} were closed --- before that it either aborted on an id, printed into a void, or measured a different corpus. \textbf {Right:} A13 as a selection diagram. $S$ switches corpus scale (12 papers $\to $ the full field); ``the pipeline completes at field scale'' is identified only if the $S$-dependence can be separated from the pipeline edge. It cannot yet: \hz {H20} was a defect whose \emph {severity} is scale-dependent (a $23\times $ waste here, ${\sim }138$k redundant rebuilds and non-termination at field scale), which is precisely an $S$-to-outcome path that does not pass through the pipeline edge. Scale transport is therefore a derivation obligation, not an extrapolation}{figure.1}{}}
